{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 17, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Raymond III Count of Tripoli Summary**\n\nRaymond III was Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III became count at a young age following his father's assassination and his mother's regency. His early reign was marked by conflicts with the Muslim leader Nur ad-Din and internal struggles with the Knights Templar.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n**Early Life and Reign**\nRaymond III was born around 1140. His father Raymond II was assassinated in 1152 and his mother Hodierna ruled as regent until he came of age. Raymond III was captured by Nur ad-Din in 1164 during the Battle of Harim. He was imprisoned for three years before being ransomed. This experience strengthened his resolve to resist Muslim advances. In 1174 he married Eschiva of Bures who brought the County of Edessa to the marriage. However Eschiva died childless in 1187.\n\n**Political Influence**\nRaymond III played a significant role in the politics of the Crusader states. He was a key figure in the faction opposing the militaristic policies of Amalric I of Jerusalem. After Amalric's death in 1174 Raymond III served as regent for the young Baldwin IV. He negotiated truces with Saladin and sought to maintain a balance of power between the Crusader states and Muslim neighbors. His regency ended in 1185 when Baldwin IV died and Guy of Lusignan became king.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n**Conflict with Guy of Lusignan**\nRaymond III opposed Guy of Lusignan’s rise to power. He supported Sibylla’s half-sister Isabella as a rival claimant to the throne. In 1186 he formed an alliance with Saladin and allowed Muslim forces to pass through his territory to attack Guy’s forces. This led to the disastrous Battle of Hattin in 1187 where Guy’s army was destroyed and Saladin captured most of the Crusader states.\n\n**Later Life and Death**\nAfter Hattin Raymond III fled to Tripoli where he died in 1187. His death marked the end of the County of Tripoli’s independence as it soon fell to Saladin. Raymond III is remembered as a pragmatic leader who sought to preserve the Crusader states through diplomacy rather than constant warfare. His policies however ultimately failed to prevent the Muslim conquest of Jerusalem and much of Outremer.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n**Legacy**\nRaymond III’s reign was defined by his efforts to navigate the complex political landscape of the Crusader states. His regency for Baldwin IV demonstrated his commitment to stability. His alliance with Saladin however remains controversial. Despite his failures Raymond III is often viewed as one of the more capable rulers of his time. His life reflects the broader struggles of the Crusader states to survive amidst internal divisions and external threats.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.17142000794410706}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.30555349588394165}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -6.12716976320371e-05}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.012767831794917583}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -6.41325386823155e-05}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -2.861018856492592e-06}, {"token": " Summary", "logprob": -0.9264675974845886}, {"token": "**\n\n", "logprob": -0.00012158608296886086}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.09469814598560333}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -2.7418097943154862e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0001408954558428377}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.14076009392738342}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.14950716495513916}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -7.748573807475623e-06}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0003725788265001029}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.0007372760446742177}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.4305012882687151e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -0.000131479